   "html_text": "If Nehamas mainly takes issue with the disavowal of desire within modern aesthetics, Richard Shusterman’s main target is the “idealist-rationalist repugnance for the body” which he finds is permeating the entire tradition of Western philosophy (2005: 324; see also Irvin (ed.) 2016). To counter this, Shusterman proposes a new discipline, “somaesthetics,” which aims to study the “experience and use of one’s body as a locus of sensory-aesthetic appreciation and creative self-fashioning” (1999: 302)",
   "md_text": "If Nehamas mainly takes issue with the disavowal of desire within modern aesthetics, Richard Shusterman’s main target is the “idealist-rationalist repugnance for the body” which he finds is permeating the entire tradition of Western philosophy (2005: 324; see also Irvin (ed.) 2016). To counter this, Shusterman proposes a new discipline, “somaesthetics,” which aims to study the “experience and use of one’s body as a locus of sensory-aesthetic appreciation and creative self-fashioning” (1999: 302)"
